# Every text file is LF in the index and in every checkout, on every platform
# (#3490). Git still detects binaries (a NUL in the first 8000 bytes) and leaves
# them alone. Before this rule, endings were pinned one extension at a time, so
# a .conf, .service, .py or extensionless script came out CRLF on a Windows
# checkout and reached a Linux node or a byte comparison with `\r` in it.
# The per-extension rules below are redundant with this one and stay for the
# incident each of them records.
# A file that must keep CRLF on purpose (a .bat, a CRLF test fixture) needs its
# own rule after this one: `<path> text eol=crlf`, or `<path> -text` to leave
# its bytes untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Line-ending policy: keep YAML (including SOPS-encrypted *.enc.yaml) as LF on
# every platform. Windows core.autocrlf=true would otherwise check these out as
# CRLF, which breaks yamllint ("wrong new line character") and churns diffs.
# CI/Linux is already LF, so this is a no-op there.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
# Markdown too: docs/lessons.md and others are edited from Windows; eol=lf keeps
# diffs to the actual content change instead of a whole-file CRLF churn.
*.md   text eol=lf
# TOOL-020: kept for any committed JS (the generated homepage-config/custom.js that
# motivated it left with Homepage, #2416) — same CRLF-churn risk as the YAML above.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.svg  text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
# Shell scripts run on Linux hosts; a CR breaks the shebang and every `case` pattern
# (SEC-037's forced command is matched byte for byte).
*.sh   text eol=lf
# Plain-text data, as LF on every platform: the watcher targets.txt files ship
# into ConfigMaps read by /bin/sh, where a CRLF checkout leaves `\r` on each
# target (#3409).
*.txt  text eol=lf
